Z||000000||Zoom88||10-19-2001||10:07 AM||||<FONT COLOR="blue"> When I bought my 88 Formula the previus owner had drilled out the idle adjustment screw, Does anyone know how to correctly adjust it back to factory specs ?<P>Also since it has started getting a little cool in the mornings my motor is idling alittle high and has started taking a little <BR>longer to get it started as well as missing slightly at idle. Any ideas as why this is happening ?</FONT><P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anyone interested in purchasing flags, flag pins as well as many other products pay a visit to my store at:<BR> <A HREF="http://198.63.2.33/?ResellerID=242937" TARGET=_blank>http://198.63.2.33/?ResellerID=242937</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Zoom88 (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||10-19-2001||11:22 AM||||The missing could be anything...bad plug, wire.  Check your timing.  Did he just drill out the plug?  Is there a screw in there?<P><BR>I just reposted this in another thread...<BR>To set idle:<BR>-Ground out terminals in ADL connector<BR>-Put key in RUN<BR>-Let sit for a minute to allow the IAC to screw in.<BR>-Unplug IAC, turn off key<BR>-Unground ADL(???I forget, but pretty sure)<BR>-Start car and adjust the Idle screw to get it to run at like 500-600rpm.<BR>-Turn off car and plug IAC back in and you should be good to go||||reg||1
Z||000002||Zoom88||10-19-2001||07:12 PM||||<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Did he just drill out the plug?  Is there a screw in there? <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <FONT COLOR="blue">Yes he drilled it out, the screw is still there, he was tinkering with the idle. My timing is good, it does not really miss, just kinda surges a little when idling tach moves back and forth around 100<BR> rpm. </FONT><P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Zoom88 (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||10-19-2001||07:28 PM||||A bunch of things could cause that.  Hows your O2 sensor?||||reg||1
